WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.530
Let me tell you about a man called Victor
Gruen.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:06.100
Born in Austria, he moved to the United States

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.839
just before the Second World War broke out

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:09.039
and became an architect.

00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:12.999
And he looked at the suburban sprawl that
was taking over America

00:00:12.999 --> 00:00:15.489
all the people just commuting back and forth,
back and forth

00:00:15.489 --> 00:00:18.600
between home and work in their cars, and thought
that what they needed

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.960
was something like the old city centres and
downtowns that he knew.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.950
What he needed was a city centre for the suburban
sprawl

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.330
and what he came up with was the idea for
the shopping mall.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.890
'Course, he had some more utopian vision for
it.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.100
He thought, oh, this is going to be wonderful,

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.920
there's going to be homes, and shops, and
schools, and even hospitals,

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.760
all working together and being this new space.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.609
Unfortunately, money and politics got in the
way of his utopian visions

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:50.179
and what you ended up with, twenty years later
when the idea crossed the Atlantic,

00:00:50.179 --> 00:00:52.519
was this: Brent Cross Shopping Centre,

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:54.879
described by a friend of mine as "Hell on
Earth on a Saturday".

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:59.309
It's the reason I've been driving around looking
for a parking space for about half an hour.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:01.609
Now, Victor Gruen did not want this.

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:04.340
He did not want these temples to consumerism

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.940
with every psychological trick in the book
designed to get you to buy.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.250
One of those psychological tricks got named
after him:

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.860
the Gruen Transfer, the idea that there are
a few seconds after you walk in

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.580
with all this new stimulus, where your brain
just gets a little bit confused

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.750
and almost hypnotises you into buying more.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.780
Doesn't matter whether it's true -- it's probably
not, the evidence is shaky,

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.950
but what matters: it was named after him.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.630
And at that point, he was railing against
what he called

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.450
these "bastard" offspring of his original
idea.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.140
Well, this is what we've ended up with.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.920
He died in 1980, at a point where there was
a new shopping mall

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.960
opening in the United States roughly every
week or so.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.630
Which was not what he wanted...

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.560
...but things have changed.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.100
Economic downturns and the rise of the internet

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.710
mean that now a lot of those old malls are
closing down.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.760
And the new spaces that are popping up in
their path?

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.310
They're a lot more like his original designs.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.060
They're pedestrianised high streets, and they're
big, open spaces

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.450
with actual space to sit down that isn't just
convincing you to buy.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:11.990
From beyond the grave, Victor Gruen might
have the last laugh.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:16.270
Not that that helps me, when that guy just
took the last parking space.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.680
